course: adult-life
lesson: 48
title: Famous people are people: privacy, scams and kind stories
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Famous people are not characters in a game; they get ill, grieve, have families, have bad days and read what is written about them.
explain: Some things are private for everyone, famous or not: health, the cause of a death, love lives, children, private messages and home addresses. Those stories belong to the person, and only they should share them.
explain: Vivid example: in Dublin, Nan reads that an old actor has died. "God rest him. Drink, they'll be saying." Mum gently: "His family haven't said why, Mam. Let's just be sorry for them." Nan nods: "You're right, love."
explain: The internet also makes things that look real but are not: AI videos, fake screenshots, forum posts with no name. And sometimes the gossip is lovely, like a big kind tip for a waitress.
fact: When someone dies, the cause is for the family to share; guessing about it hurts people who are grieving.
fact: One mimed song on a TV show, where many performers mime, does not prove a singer cannot sing.
fact: Influencers must say when a post is a paid advert; asking "is someone paid to say this?" protects you.
fact: "Musical differences" and "taking a break" mean only what was said; nobody outside the band knows the rest.
fact: When someone says they are hurt by comments, the kind thing is to listen, not to argue.
fact: An anonymous forum post is not news, and children's lives should never be dragged into gossip.
fact: A few seconds of video, like a tired "not now" to a selfie, cannot show what a whole person is like.
fact: AI can now make fake videos of real people saying things they never said; check whether the person really said it.
fact: Screenshots of messages can be faked in seconds, and private messages are private even if they are real.
fact: Child YouTubers are still children; their money and homes are private and they deserve protection.
fact: Until the film makers announce it, who plays James Bond is only guessing by fans and betting sites.
fact: Keeping a wedding private by choice is not the same as hiding something bad.
fact: Online cruelty drives real people off social media; memes mocking someone's looks are bullying, even when lots of people share them.
fact: Kind stories spread too, and they are the nicest sort of gossip.
exercise: An actor has died; his family gave no cause. Nan: "They're saying it was drink." What's the kind response? | Say you're sorry, and don't guess what killed him | Agree, because famous people usually drink far too much | Look up the reason on a forum | Ask his family on social media
exercise: A singer mimed one song on a TV show. Kids say, "She can't sing!" What's fair? | One mimed song doesn't show she can't sing | Miming always means a singer has no real voice at all | TV shows never let anyone mime | She must have lost her voice for good
exercise: An influencer sold a "miracle" cream and didn't say she was paid. What should you ask next time? | Is someone paid to say this? | Is the cream in a pretty jar? | How many likes does the post have? | Is the influencer good-looking?
exercise: A band says it's "taking a break" over "musical differences". Dad: "They hate each other." What's actually known? | Only that they are taking a break | That they had a big fist fight | That they will never play again | That the drummer started it all
exercise: An actress writes that comments about her weight hurt her. What's the kind response? | Listen, and don't argue with her feelings | Tell her famous people must expect it and get used to it | Post more comments to cheer her up | Say she should have kept it to herself
exercise: Auntie Jen read on a forum that a presenter has a "secret love child". What is it? | An anonymous claim with no evidence | Confirmed news from the presenter's own mouth | A report checked by a court | A story from a trusted newspaper
exercise: A video shows a footballer saying "not now" to a fan's selfie while walking to the bus. Is he mean? | You can't tell from a few busy seconds | Yes, he hates all of his fans | Yes, footballers must take every selfie | No, the fan was being very rude
exercise: An AI video shows an actor praising a product, and he says it's fake. What is the lesson? | Videos can be faked; check if he really said it | If it looks real, then it must be real, so believe it | AI videos are always clearly cartoons | Actors often forget what they said
exercise: A screenshot shows a model's boyfriend "cheating". What should you remember first? | Screenshots are easy to fake and easy to share | Screenshots are the strongest proof there is of anything | Sharing it helps the model find the truth | It must be true if it went viral
exercise: Kids say an eleven-year-old YouTuber has a mansion and a Ferrari. The Ferrari was hired for one video. What should we remember? | He is still a child and deserves protection | He is rich, so he needs no protection from anyone | He must have bought the Ferrari himself | Child YouTubers are not real children
exercise: "That actor's going to be the next James Bond!" The film makers have said nothing. What is it? | Fans' guessing, not an announcement | An official news report from the studio | A secret the actor confirmed | A fact because it's all over the internet
exercise: A singer married on a beach with only family there and posted photos afterwards. "In secret!" says Auntie Jen. What's fair? | Keeping it private is not hiding something bad | She must have been ashamed of the wedding and the groom | Weddings must be announced in advance | She tricked her own family into coming
exercise: Someone made a meme about a TV cook's teeth and she stopped posting. What is the meme? | Bullying, even if lots of people share it | Harmless fun, because she is famous and rich | News that the public needs to know | Advice that helped her with her teeth
exercise: An actor tips a waitress a thousand pounds and it's in the news. What kind of gossip is this? | A kind story, the nicest sort of gossip | A scandal that should be kept hidden | A rumour that hurts the waitress | A scam to steal the waitress's money
exercise: A royal-watcher on YouTube says she "knows everything inside the palace". How likely is that? | Very unlikely; most just read the papers and guess | Very likely, YouTubers have palace passes | Certain, because she has many viewers | Certain, because she speaks very confidently and knows names
exercise: A sports star stood quietly during the anthem. Big brother: "Disrespectful!" What's fairer? | People show respect in different ways | Standing quietly is against the law | He must hate his own country | He forgot the words, so it's fine
test: Big brother: "Celeb got kicked off a plane for being drunk and rude, a passenger posted it!" The airline only said "disruptive passenger". What should you do? | Wait for both sides before deciding | Share the post, passengers always know | Decide he was drunk because he's famous | Blame the airline for the whole thing
test: Auntie Bev: "Those two telly sisters haven't spoken in ten years, everyone knows." They only said it was "difficult". What's true? | The "ten years" part was added by the papers | They've said in public they hate each other forever | They have never met each other at all | The papers asked both and they agreed
test: Kids at school: "Her new album's out TOMORROW, a leak said!" The record company has said nothing. What is the "leak"? | Probably a fan's wish with a cool name | A secret message from the singer | An official date from the record company | A fact because the word "leak" was used
test: Mum, sighing: "That actress quit social media cos of the hate, and some say she's too sensitive." What is the lesson? | Online cruelty drives real people away | She should have just ignored it all | Famous people are not allowed to feel hurt | Being sensitive is the real problem here
test: Your mate: "that chef puts bogeys in the food, my mate's uncle worked there, swear." What is it most likely? | A disgusting made-up story that spreads because it shocks | A health inspector's report from last week | A true story because his uncle said it and he would know | A joke the chef tells on his own show
test: Grandad: "That writer's broke now, living in a caravan, I heard." Nothing has been confirmed. What kind of story is it? | A sad-sounding rumour about something private | A fact from the writer's bank | Good news the writer shared himself | A story checked by the newspapers
test: Your sister wants to forward a screenshot of a model's "cheating" boyfriend: "it's so juicy lol." What's the best reply? | It could be fake, and private messages aren't ours to share | Send it to everyone, because the truth must always come out | Post it with a funny caption first | Only send it to people who like the model
test: Nan: "That YouTube lady knows all the palace secrets, you know, she said so." Put Nan right, kindly. | Most of those channels just read the papers and guess | Yes, she works inside the palace every day | Yes, the King tells her the news first | No, she's actually a member of the royal family
test: Little brother sees an AI video of an actor selling sweets: "he said it so it's true!" What do you tell him? | The video can be fake; check if he really said it | If his face is in it, then he said it | AI can only make videos of cartoons | Actors always sell sweets in their adverts, so it is fine
test: Big brother: "Ha, look at this meme of the cook's teeth, sending it to the chat." What happens if he does? | He joins in bullying a real person | He helps the cook get famous again | Nothing, memes don't count as anything | The cook will be pleased to see it
test: Dad reads: "Actor gives waitress a £1,000 tip." Mum smiles: "Now that's gossip I like." Why? | It's a kind story that hurts nobody | It proves the actor is very rich | It's a secret the waitress wanted kept | It's a scandal about the restaurant
exam: Identify the party to whom the disclosure of the cause of a person's death properly belongs. | The family of the deceased | The readers of popular newspapers | Any person present at the funeral | The operators of internet forums
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning video recordings produced by artificial intelligence in which a public figure appears to endorse a product. | Such recordings may be entirely fabricated without the figure's consent | Such recordings constitute conclusive proof of endorsement | Such recordings cannot at present be produced convincingly | Such recordings are produced solely by the figures themselves
exam: Whereas a person engaged to promote a product receives payment for doing so, determine the obligation imposed upon that person by the advertising rules. | To disclose that the communication is an advertisement | To refrain from using the product personally | To promote solely products manufactured abroad | To obtain the written consent of every follower in advance
exam: Ascertain the classification appropriate to an image, widely circulated, which ridicules the physical appearance of a television presenter. | Bullying, irrespective of the number who share it | Legitimate news of public importance | Constructive advice concerning appearance | Harmless entertainment, given the subject's fame
